Prefix Delegation
❗ The Problem Before Prefix Delegation

Traditionally, each Pod in EKS gets an ENI secondary IP from the VPC subnet.

Example:

One EC2 node may support only max 30 Pods because it can only hold ~40 IPs per ENI.

IP exhaustion often happens in large clusters.

This limits pod density.

✅ How Prefix Delegation Fixes It

With Prefix Delegation:

Instead of giving the node 20–30 individual IPs

AWS gives the node an entire /28 block (16 IPs) as a single assignment.

A single ENI can receive multiple prefixes.

One prefix = 16 IPs

Out of which:

5 IPs are reserved

11 usable Pod IPs

If the node gets 4 prefixes, that is 44 usable Pod IPs per ENI.

ENI:
  Secondary IPs:
    - 10.0.1.21
    - 10.0.1.22
    - ...
ENI:
  IPv4 Prefix:
    - 10.0.2.0/28
    - 10.0.3.0/28


This massively increases pod density.
